{#
  Every evaluator's verdict and comment, at the bottom of an evaluation.

  Expects:
    reviews  — EvaluationReview[] from findForEvaluation(), newest activity first
    myReview — this user's own, so theirs can be marked as such

  ── Why reviews WITHOUT a comment still appear ─────────────────────────────
  The heading says "comments", but a verdict recorded with no prose is still
  something a reader needs to see: a list that quietly dropped them would not
  add up to the tally in the banner at the top, and "2 agree" next to a list
  showing one entry reads as a bug. The verdict is the record; the comment is
  an optional annotation on it.

  ── Why <article> and not <li> ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  Each entry is independently meaningful — attribution, verdict, reason, prose,
  timestamp — which is exactly what <article> is for, and it gives screen
  reader users something to navigate between. The <h3> inside each keeps the
  document outline going h1 (evaluation) → h2 (this section) → h3 (one review)
  without a level being skipped.
#}
<section class="review-list" aria-labelledby="review-comments-heading">
  <h2 id="review-comments-heading">Evaluator comments ({{ reviews|length }})</h2>

  {% if reviews is empty %}
    <p class="muted">Nobody has reviewed this evaluation yet.</p>
  {% else %}
    {% for review in reviews %}
      {% set mine = myReview and myReview.id == review.id %}
      <article class="review{{ mine ? ' review--mine' : '' }}">
        <h3 class="review-byline">
          {{ review.reviewer.displayName }}
          {% if mine %}<span class="muted">(you)</span>{% endif %}
          {#
            The badge repeats what the text beside it already says. The colour is
            never the message: "Agree" and "Disagree" are unmistakable words, and
            green-vs-red is the one pairing guaranteed to collapse for a reader
            with deuteranopia — the same reasoning as the match-count tiles.
          #}
          <span class="badge badge--{{ review.verdict.value }}">{{ review.verdict.shortLabel }}</span>
        </h3>

        {#
          <time> with a machine-readable datetime, so the date is available to
          anything parsing the page rather than only to a human reading it.
          recordedAt is "updated if edited, otherwise created" — see the entity.
        #}
        <p class="review-meta muted">
          <time datetime="{{ review.recordedAt|date('c') }}">{{ review.recordedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}</time>
          {%- if review.wasEdited %} · edited{% endif %}
          {%- if review.reasonLabel %} · Reason: {{ review.reasonLabel }}
          {%- elseif review.verdict.requiresReason %} · <span class="muted">reason no longer available</span>
          {%- endif %}
        </p>

        {#
          Twig autoescapes, so the comment cannot inject markup. nl2br is applied
          because evaluators type line breaks and HTML would otherwise collapse
          them into one paragraph — it runs AFTER escaping, which is the safe
          order and the one Twig's filter chain gives us for free here.
        #}
        {% if review.hasComments %}
          <p class="review-comment">{{ review.comments|nl2br }}</p>
        {% else %}
          <p class="review-comment muted">No comment given.</p>
        {% endif %}
      </article>
    {% endfor %}
  {% endif %}
</section>
